<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mesha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> · Also known as King Mesha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mesha was king of Moab in the 9th century BCE, known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and his own inscription, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesha-stele">Mesha Stele</a>. The son of Chemosh-yatti, he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhiban">Dibon</a> and threw off the domination of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> after the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahab">Ahab</a>, recovering territory north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnon">Arnon</a> and building towns, roads and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pararaton">Book of Kings</a> tells how an Israelite campaign against him failed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mesha.jpg" alt="Mesha" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Sivonen from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mesha_Stele_(511142469).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
